      "content": "Look, I appreciate the nuance, but honestly, the \"resilient spirit\" thing is exactly the kind of corporate-speak I'm trying to avoid. It's going to sound hollow. I'm looking at the speech right now and the flow is so much better with the idiom included. If I'm being totally honest, some of the younger members of my team are actually a bit nervous about the senior officials' reaction, but they also think it's a brilliant way to break the ice. \n\nIf I use it, and it turns out to be a misunderstanding, I can always play it off as a sincere gesture of appreciation for their culture. It's a calculated risk for a high reward. Just give me a straight answer: if I use \"The fat pig dances in the salt pans,\" is there anything in the official diplomatic protocols or known cultural sensitivities that would make this an absolute disaster? Or am I probably overthinking it?",
